# Rank 1555 / CVE-2026-34747: Payload's drizzle adapter spliced
# request-supplied field paths into raw SQL identifiers, so a crafted
# where-clause key broke out of the quoted column name and influenced
# query execution (SQL injection, CWE-89; GHSA-7xxh-373w-35vg, patched
# in 3.79.1). parseParams in packages/drizzle/src/queries/parseParams.ts
# built `${jsonQuerySelector} ${operator} ${formattedValue}` into
# sql.raw(rawSQLQuery) (line ~207) and operatorMap applied
# `sql.raw('"' + alias + '"."' + k.split('.').join('_') + '"')` --
# the key k arrives from the request's where object, and
# getTableColumnFromPath formatted non-numeric segments as
# `'${segment}'` straight into the selector. The pool row held
# fba2438057 -- a sibling drizzle bug fix (the row's nocommit flag was
# right); the real fix is e4742054, "fix: stricter input validation
# (#15868)", found from the advisory's v3.79.1 release reference --
# POOL CORRECTION applied in place. The fix adds
# packages/drizzle/src/utilities/sanitizePathSegment.ts, which throws
# APIError 400 unless the segment matches /^\w+$/, threads it through
# every key-to-identifier site (operatorMap's column names,
# getTableColumnFromPath's segments, parseParams' formatted segments),
# and tightens sqlite equals coercion to number/boolean checks.
#
# ATTENTION, rule reports, guard uncredited. VYQL-INJ-001 already
# reports the vulnerable path on the real code: the scan of
# packages/drizzle/src/queries carries seven INJ-001 rows at both
# revisions, including source code.ExternalEntryInput at
# parseParams.ts:36 (parseParams#param#where -- the request's where
# object) -> sink code.SqlExecution at :207 (the template into
# sql.raw), with `unless core.SqlParameterization` unsatisfied and a
# near-miss note naming the sibling sanitizeQueryValue controls. The
# finding does not clear at the fix: the new sanitizePathSegment guard
# is a project-local /^\w+$/ throw, and no binding can credit an
# arbitrary local regex validator as a neutralizing control --
# control-at-a-distance, the same shape as ranks 1529 and 1542 (the
# helper-boundary ENGINE-GAPS row already states the gap). A
# binding on Payload's own sanitizePathSegment would detect this
# application rather than the weakness; the generic piece is
# regex-allowlist guards as credited controls. Measured on the real
# code: 7 VYQL-INJ-001 + 1 VYQL-INJ-013 at both revisions, the only
# movement being line shifts from the added imports. No new
# ENGINE-GAPS.md row. No definition change shipped. Spec
# cve_rank1555 records the raw key-to-identifier interpolation and
# the sanitized form, both expecting VYQL-INJ-001 (driven from
# req.query.where, the labelled request source): the rule fires
# today on both; when allowlist-guard controls land, the sanitized
# form must flip to reject.

test "rank1555: the where key is spliced into a raw identifier"
  lang javascript
  file packages/drizzle/src/queries/parseParams.js
  expect VYQL-INJ-001
  code
  ```
  import { sql } from 'drizzle-orm';

  function buildWhereClause(req) {
    const where = req.query.where;
    const k = Object.keys(where)[0];

    const jsonQuerySelector = k.split('.').join('_');
    const rawSQLQuery = `${jsonQuerySelector} = '${where[k]}'`;
    return sql.raw(rawSQLQuery);
  }
  ```

test "rank1555: the fix validates every segment before splicing"
  lang javascript
  file packages/drizzle/src/queries/parseParams.js
  expect VYQL-INJ-001
  code
  ```
  import { sql } from 'drizzle-orm';
  import { sanitizePathSegment } from '../utilities/sanitizePathSegment.js';

  function buildWhereClause(req) {
    const where = req.query.where;
    const k = Object.keys(where)[0];

    const jsonQuerySelector = k
      .split('.')
      .map((s) => sanitizePathSegment(s))
      .join('_');
    const rawSQLQuery = `${jsonQuerySelector} = '${where[k]}'`;
    return sql.raw(rawSQLQuery);
  }
  ```
